            "question": "{\"content\":\"<style>\\n    /* 1920. */\\n    .text-container {\\n      cursor: pointer;\\n      max-width: 20px;\\n      white-space: normal; /* Allow the text to wrap */\\n      overflow: hidden;\\n      position: relative;\\n      line-height: 1.4; /* Adjust for better readability */\\n      transition: max-width 0.3s ease-in-out, max-height 0.3s ease-in-out; /* Smooth transition for expanding */\\n      max-height: 1.4em; /* Set to line height to show only the first line */\\n    }\\n    .text-container::after {\\n      content: '  ...  ';\\n      position: absolute;\\n      right: 0;\\n      bottom: 0;\\n      color: #d5d5d5;\\n      background:  #f0f0f0;\\n      width: 100%;\\n      text-align: right;\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover {\\n      max-width: 350px; /* Adjust as needed for hover */\\n      max-height: none; /* Allow it to expand to fit content */\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover::after {\\n      display: none; /* Hide the ellipsis on hover */\\n    }\\n    a:link, a:visited {\\n        color: black;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n<span><b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/build-array-from-permutation\\\">1920. Build Array from Permutation</a></b></span>\\n<div>Construct an array based on a zero-based permutation.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1. Given an array `nums`, which is a zero-based permutation of distinct integers from 0 to `nums.length - 1`.<br/>2. Build an array `ans` of the same length as `nums`.<br/>3. For each index `i` in `nums`, `ans[i]` should be equal to `nums[nums[i]]`.<br/>4. Return the constructed array `ans`.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>[5,0,1,2,3,4] => [4,5,0,1,2,3]</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>Example 1:<br/><br/>Input: nums = [0,2,1,5,3,4]<br/>Output: [0,1,2,4,5,3]<br/>Explanation: The array ans is built as follows: <br/>ans = [nums[nums[0]], nums[nums[1]], nums[nums[2]], nums[nums[3]], nums[nums[4]], nums[nums[5]]]<br/>    = [nums[0], nums[2], nums[1], nums[5], nums[3], nums[4]]<br/>    = [0,1,2,4,5,3]<br/><br/>Example 2:<br/><br/>Input: nums = [5,0,1,2,3,4]<br/>Output: [4,5,0,1,2,3]<br/>Explanation: The array ans is built as follows:<br/>ans = [nums[nums[0]], nums[nums[1]], nums[nums[2]], nums[nums[3]], nums[nums[4]], nums[nums[5]]]<br/>    = [nums[5], nums[0], nums[1], nums[2], nums[3], nums[4]]<br/>    = [4,5,0,1,2,3]<br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1 <= nums.length <= 1000<br/>0 <= nums[i] < nums.length<br/>The elements in nums are distinct.<br/><br/> <br/><br/>Follow-up: Can you solve it without using an extra space (i.e., O(1) memory)?<br/></div>\\n<span><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/build-array-from-permutation/solutions/\\\">[1]</a></span>\"}",
            "answer": "{\"content\":\"<b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/build-array-from-permutation/solutions/1362485/javascript-easy-solution/\\\">Array Mapping</a></b>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>- No additional variables needed beyond the input array `nums`.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>return nums.map(a => nums[a]);</div>\\n<div>1. Use the `map` function to iterate over each element in `nums`.<br/>2. For each element `a` in `nums`, map it to `nums[a]`.<br/>3. Return the new mapped array.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>The solution involves mapping each element of the array `nums` to a new value based on the permutation rule provided, thereby constructing the new array.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    // Alternative approach using a for loop<br/>\\n    var buildArrayAlt = function(nums) {<br/>\\n        let ans = new Array(nums.length);<br/>\\n        for (let i = 0; i ＜ nums.length; i++) {<br/>\\n            ans[i] = nums[nums[i]];<br/>\\n        }<br/>\\n        return ans;<br/>\\n    };<br/><br/>\\n    This alternative solution uses a traditional `for` loop to construct the array, which provides a more explicit approach to the problem compared to the concise `map` method.<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>O(n), where n is the length of the array `nums`.</div>\"}",
