            "question": "{\"content\":\"<style>\\n    /* 2942. */\\n    .text-container {\\n      cursor: pointer;\\n      max-width: 20px;\\n      white-space: normal; /* Allow the text to wrap */\\n      overflow: hidden;\\n      position: relative;\\n      line-height: 1.4; /* Adjust for better readability */\\n      transition: max-width 0.3s ease-in-out, max-height 0.3s ease-in-out; /* Smooth transition for expanding */\\n      max-height: 1.4em; /* Set to line height to show only the first line */\\n    }\\n    .text-container::after {\\n      content: '  ...  ';\\n      position: absolute;\\n      right: 0;\\n      bottom: 0;\\n      color: #d5d5d5;\\n      background:  #f0f0f0;\\n      width: 100%;\\n      text-align: right;\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover {\\n      max-width: 350px; /* Adjust as needed for hover */\\n      max-height: none; /* Allow it to expand to fit content */\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover::after {\\n      display: none; /* Hide the ellipsis on hover */\\n    }\\n    a:link, a:visited {\\n        color: black;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n<span><b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/find-words-containing-character\\\">2942. Find Words Containing Character</a></b></span>\\n<div>Identify indices of words in an array that contain a specific character.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1. Given an array of strings `words` and a character `x`.<br/>2. The task is to find all indices in `words` where each word contains the character `x`.<br/>3. The character `x` can be present in any position within a word.<br/>4. The order of indices in the output does not matter.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>Input: `words = [\\\"abc\\\", \\\"bcd\\\", \\\"aaaa\\\", \\\"cbc\\\"]`, `x = \\\"a\\\"` => Output: `[0, 2]`</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>Example 1:<br/><br/>Input: words = [\\\"leet\\\",\\\"code\\\"], x = \\\"e\\\"<br/>Output: [0,1]<br/>Explanation: \\\"e\\\" occurs in both words: \\\"leet\\\", and \\\"code\\\". Hence, we return indices 0 and 1.<br/><br/><br/>Example 2:<br/><br/>Input: words = [\\\"abc\\\",\\\"bcd\\\",\\\"aaaa\\\",\\\"cbc\\\"], x = \\\"a\\\"<br/>Output: [0,2]<br/>Explanation: \\\"a\\\" occurs in \\\"abc\\\", and \\\"aaaa\\\". Hence, we return indices 0 and 2.<br/><br/><br/>Example 3:<br/><br/>Input: words = [\\\"abc\\\",\\\"bcd\\\",\\\"aaaa\\\",\\\"cbc\\\"], x = \\\"z\\\"<br/>Output: []<br/>Explanation: \\\"z\\\" does not occur in any of the words. Hence, we return an empty array.<br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1 <= words.length <= 50<br/>1 <= words[i].length <= 50<br/>x is a lowercase English letter.<br/>words[i] consists only of lowercase English letters.<br/></div>\\n<span><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/find-words-containing-character/solutions/\\\">[1]</a></span>\",\"media\":[]}",
            "answer": "{\"content\":\"<b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/find-words-containing-character/solutions/4335330/one-liner-reduce/\\\">FlatMap for array iteration and filtering.</a></b>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    // Setup: Define initial variables<br/>\\n    let words = [\\\"apple\\\", \\\"banana\\\", \\\"cherry\\\"]; // Example array of words<br/>\\n    let x = 'a'; // Character to search for<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div>\\n    // Main Solution Code: Using flatMap for iteration and filtering<br/>\\n    function(words, x) {<br/>\\n        return words.flatMap((word, index) => word.includes(x) ? [index] : []);<br/>\\n    }<br/>\\n</div>\\n<div>1. Iterate over each word in the array `words`.<br/>2. For each word, check if it contains the character `x`.<br/>3. If it contains `x`, return the current index; otherwise, return an empty array.<br/>4. The flatMap method will flatten the resultant array of arrays into a single array containing only the indices where the word contains `x`.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    // Description: Explaining the logic and approach<br/>\\n    Use flatMap to efficiently iterate and conditionally return indices of words containing the specified character.<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    // Alternative Solution: Iterative approach<br/>\\n    function findWordsContaining(words, x) {<br/>\\n        let indices = [];<br/>\\n        for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {<br/>\\n            if (words[i].includes(x)) {<br/>\\n                indices.push(i);<br/>\\n            }<br/>\\n        }<br/>\\n        return indices;<br/>\\n    }<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>- O(n) where n is the number of words in the array.</div>\",\"media\":[]}",
