            "question": "{\"content\":\"<style>\\n    /* 2114. */\\n    .text-container {\\n      cursor: pointer;\\n      max-width: 20px;\\n      white-space: normal; /* Allow the text to wrap */\\n      overflow: hidden;\\n      position: relative;\\n      line-height: 1.4; /* Adjust for better readability */\\n      transition: max-width 0.3s ease-in-out, max-height 0.3s ease-in-out; /* Smooth transition for expanding */\\n      max-height: 1.4em; /* Set to line height to show only the first line */\\n    }\\n    .text-container::after {\\n      content: '  ...  ';\\n      position: absolute;\\n      right: 0;\\n      bottom: 0;\\n      color: #d5d5d5;\\n      background:  #f0f0f0;\\n      width: 100%;\\n      text-align: right;\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover {\\n      max-width: 350px; /* Adjust as needed for hover */\\n      max-height: none; /* Allow it to expand to fit content */\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover::after {\\n      display: none; /* Hide the ellipsis on hover */\\n    }\\n    a:link, a:visited {\\n        color: black;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n<span><b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-number-of-words-found-in-sentences\\\">2114. Maximum Number of Words Found in Sentences</a></b></span>\\n<div>Find the maximum number of words in any single sentence from a list of sentences.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1. Given an array `sentences`, where each element is a string representing a single sentence.<br/>2. Each sentence consists of words separated by a single space, with no leading or trailing spaces.<br/>3. The task is to determine the sentence with the most words and return the count of words in that sentence.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>Input: `sentences = [\\\"please wait\\\", \\\"continue to fight\\\", \\\"continue to win\\\"]` => Output: `3`</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>Example 1:<br/><br/>Input: sentences = [\\\"alice and bob love leetcode\\\", \\\"i think so too\\\", \\\"this is great thanks very much\\\"]<br/>Output: 6<br/>Explanation: <br/>- The first sentence, \\\"alice and bob love leetcode\\\", has 5 words in total.<br/>- The second sentence, \\\"i think so too\\\", has 4 words in total.<br/>- The third sentence, \\\"this is great thanks very much\\\", has 6 words in total.<br/>Thus, the maximum number of words in a single sentence comes from the third sentence, which has 6 words.<br/><br/><br/>Example 2:<br/><br/>Input: sentences = [\\\"please wait\\\", \\\"continue to fight\\\", \\\"continue to win\\\"]<br/>Output: 3<br/>Explanation: It is possible that multiple sentences contain the same number of words. <br/>In this example, the second and third sentences (underlined) have the same number of words.<br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1 <= sentences.length <= 100<br/>1 <= sentences[i].length <= 100<br/>sentences[i] consists only of lowercase English letters and ' ' only.<br/>sentences[i] does not have leading or trailing spaces.<br/>All the words in sentences[i] are separated by a single space.<br/></div>\\n<span><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-number-of-words-found-in-sentences/solutions/\\\">[1]</a></span>\"}",
            "answer": "{\"content\":\"<b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-number-of-words-found-in-sentences/solutions/1683428/javascript-solution/\\\">Linear traversal and string manipulation (splitting).</a></b>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    // Setup: Define initial variables<br/>\\n    let sentences = [\\\"This is a sentence\\\", \\\"This is another longer sentence\\\", \\\"Short\\\"]; // Example sentences array<br/>\\n    let max = 0; // Integer to track the maximum word count<br/>\\n    let temp = 0; // Temporary integer to hold the word count of the current sentence<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div>\\n    // Main Solution Code: Using linear traversal and string splitting<br/>\\n    for (let i = 0; i ＜ sentences.length; i++) {<br/>\\n        temp = sentences[i].split(\\\" \\\").length;<br/>\\n        if (temp > max) {<br/>\\n            max = temp;<br/>\\n        }<br/>\\n    }<br/>\\n    return max;<br/>\\n</div>\\n<div>1. Initialize a variable `max` to keep track of the maximum word count.<br/>2. Iterate through each sentence in the `sentences` array.<br/>3. For each sentence, split it into words using space as the delimiter and count the number of words.<br/>4. If the count of words in the current sentence is greater than `max`, update `max`.<br/>5. After iterating through all sentences, return `max` as the result.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    // Description: Explaining the logic and approach<br/>\\n    Iterate over each sentence, split it into words, and keep track of the maximum word count.<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    // Alternative Solution: Different approach for the problem<br/>\\n    function mostWordsFound(sentences) {<br/>\\n        let maxWords = 0;<br/>\\n        for (const sentence of sentences) {<br/>\\n            const wordCount = sentence.split(' ').length;<br/>\\n            maxWords = Math.max(maxWords, wordCount);<br/>\\n        }<br/>\\n        return maxWords;<br/>\\n    }<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>O(n * m) where n is the number of sentences and m is the average length of each sentence.</div>\"}",
