            "question": "{\"content\":\"<style>\\n    /* 654. */\\n    .text-container {\\n      cursor: pointer;\\n      max-width: 20px;\\n      white-space: normal; /* Allow the text to wrap */\\n      overflow: hidden;\\n      position: relative;\\n      line-height: 1.4; /* Adjust for better readability */\\n      transition: max-width 0.3s ease-in-out, max-height 0.3s ease-in-out; /* Smooth transition for expanding */\\n      max-height: 1.4em; /* Set to line height to show only the first line */\\n    }\\n    .text-container::after {\\n      content: '  ...  ';\\n      position: absolute;\\n      right: 0;\\n      bottom: 0;\\n      color: #d5d5d5;\\n      background:  #f0f0f0;\\n      width: 100%;\\n      text-align: right;\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover {\\n      max-width: 350px; /* Adjust as needed for hover */\\n      max-height: none; /* Allow it to expand to fit content */\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover::after {\\n      display: none; /* Hide the ellipsis on hover */\\n    }\\n    a:link, a:visited {\\n        color: black;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n<span><b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-binary-tree\\\">654. Maximum Binary Tree</a></b></span>\\n<div>Construct a maximum binary tree from an integer array without duplicates using a specific recursive algorithm.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1. Given an integer array `nums` with no duplicates.<br/>2. Build a maximum binary tree:<br/>   - The root node's value is the maximum value in `nums`.<br/>   - Recursively build the left subtree using the elements to the left of the maximum value.<br/>   - Recursively build the right subtree using the elements to the right of the maximum value.<br/>3. Return the root node of the constructed binary tree.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>`nums = [3,2,1,6,0,5]` => `[6,3,5,null,2,0,null,null,1]`</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>Example 1:<br/><br/>Input: nums = [3,2,1,6,0,5]<br/>Output: [6,3,5,null,2,0,null,null,1]<br/>Explanation: The recursive calls are as follow:<br/>- The largest value in [3,2,1,6,0,5] is 6. Left prefix is [3,2,1] and right suffix is [0,5].<br/>    - The largest value in [3,2,1] is 3. Left prefix is [] and right suffix is [2,1].<br/>        - Empty array, so no child.<br/>        - The largest value in [2,1] is 2. Left prefix is [] and right suffix is [1].<br/>            - Empty array, so no child.<br/>            - Only one element, so child is a node with value 1.<br/>    - The largest value in [0,5] is 5. Left prefix is [0] and right suffix is [].<br/>        - Only one element, so child is a node with value 0.<br/>        - Empty array, so no child.<br/><br/><br/>Example 2:<br/><br/>Input: nums = [3,2,1]<br/>Output: [3,null,2,null,1]<br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1 <= nums.length <= 1000<br/>0 <= nums[i] <= 1000<br/>All integers in nums are unique.<br/></div>\\n<span><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-binary-tree/solutions/\\\">[1]</a></span>\"}",
            "answer": "{\"content\":\"<b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-binary-tree/solutions/428478/simple-recursive-javascript-solution/\\\">Recursive Tree Construction</a></b>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    // SETUP<br/>\\n    let nums = [/* array of numbers */]; // Array of integers<br/>\\n    function TreeNode(val, left, right) {<br/>\\n        this.val = (val === undefined ? 0 : val);<br/>\\n        this.left = (left === undefined ? null : left);<br/>\\n        this.right = (right === undefined ? null : right);<br/>\\n    }<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div>\\n    function constructMaximumBinaryTree(nums) {<br/>\\n        if (nums.length === 0) return null;<br/>\\n        let max = Math.max(...nums);<br/>\\n        let index = nums.indexOf(max);<br/>\\n        let head = new TreeNode(max);<br/>\\n        head.left = constructMaximumBinaryTree(nums.slice(0, index));<br/>\\n        head.right = constructMaximumBinaryTree(nums.slice(index + 1));<br/>\\n        return head;<br/>\\n    }<br/>\\n</div>\\n<div>\\n    1. Check if the `nums` array is empty; if yes, return null.<br/>\\n    2. Find the maximum value in `nums` and its index.<br/>\\n    3. Create a new tree node with the maximum value as the root.<br/>\\n    4. Assign the left child of the root by recursively calling the function with the left subarray of `nums`.<br/>\\n    5. Assign the right child of the root by recursively calling the function with the right subarray of `nums`.<br/>\\n    6. Return the root node.<br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    The solution constructs a binary tree such that each node is the maximum value of the subarray it represents, with left and right subtrees constructed recursively.<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    // This alternative solution uses a similar approach but iterates over the array to find the maximum value and its index without using built-in functions like Math.max and indexOf<br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>The time complexity is O(n^2) in the worst case due to the `slice` operation in each recursive call.<br/><br/></div>\"}",
