            "question": "{\"content\":\"<style>\\n    /* 1662. */\\n    .text-container {\\n      cursor: pointer;\\n      max-width: 20px;\\n      white-space: normal; /* Allow the text to wrap */\\n      overflow: hidden;\\n      position: relative;\\n      line-height: 1.4; /* Adjust for better readability */\\n      transition: max-width 0.3s ease-in-out, max-height 0.3s ease-in-out; /* Smooth transition for expanding */\\n      max-height: 1.4em; /* Set to line height to show only the first line */\\n    }\\n    .text-container::after {\\n      content: '  ...  ';\\n      position: absolute;\\n      right: 0;\\n      bottom: 0;\\n      color: #d5d5d5;\\n      background:  #f0f0f0;\\n      width: 100%;\\n      text-align: right;\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover {\\n      max-width: 350px; /* Adjust as needed for hover */\\n      max-height: none; /* Allow it to expand to fit content */\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover::after {\\n      display: none; /* Hide the ellipsis on hover */\\n    }\\n    a:link, a:visited {\\n        color: black;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n<span><b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/check-if-two-string-arrays-are-equivalent\\\">1662. Check If Two String Arrays are Equivalent</a></b></span>\\n<div>Determine if two string arrays represent the same string when their elements are concatenated.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1. Given two string arrays `word1` and `word2`.<br/>2. The arrays represent strings formed by concatenating their elements.<br/>3. Check if the strings represented by `word1` and `word2` are the same.<br/>4. Return `true` if they represent the same string, otherwise return `false`.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>`word1 = [\\\"ab\\\", \\\"c\\\"], word2 = [\\\"a\\\", \\\"bc\\\"]` => `true`</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>Example 1:<br/><br/>Input: word1 = [\\\"ab\\\", \\\"c\\\"], word2 = [\\\"a\\\", \\\"bc\\\"]<br/>Output: true<br/>Explanation:<br/>word1 represents string \\\"ab\\\" + \\\"c\\\" -> \\\"abc\\\"<br/>word2 represents string \\\"a\\\" + \\\"bc\\\" -> \\\"abc\\\"<br/>The strings are the same, so return true.<br/><br/>Example 2:<br/><br/>Input: word1 = [\\\"a\\\", \\\"cb\\\"], word2 = [\\\"ab\\\", \\\"c\\\"]<br/>Output: false<br/><br/><br/>Example 3:<br/><br/>Input: word1  = [\\\"abc\\\", \\\"d\\\", \\\"defg\\\"], word2 = [\\\"abcddefg\\\"]<br/>Output: true<br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1 <= word1.length, word2.length <= 103<br/>1 <= word1[i].length, word2[i].length <= 103<br/>1 <= sum(word1[i].length), sum(word2[i].length) <= 103<br/>word1[i] and word2[i] consist of lowercase letters.<br/></div>\\n<span><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/check-if-two-string-arrays-are-equivalent/solutions/\\\">[1]</a></span>\"}",
            "answer": "{\"content\":\"<b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/check-if-two-string-arrays-are-equivalent/solutions/1007247/javascript-1-liner/\\\">String Comparison</a></b>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    let word1 = ['...', '...']; // Array of strings representing the first word<br/>\\n    let word2 = ['...', '...']; // Array of strings representing the second word<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div>\\n    return word1.join('') === word2.join('');<br/>\\n</div>\\n<div>1. Concatenate all elements of `word1` into a single string.<br/>2. Concatenate all elements of `word2` into another single string.<br/>3. Compare the two resulting strings for equality.<br/>4. Return `true` if they are equal, else `false`.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    The solution involves concatenating the arrays into strings and then comparing these strings for equality.<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>\\n    let concatenatedWord1 = '', concatenatedWord2 = '';<br/>\\n    for (let i = 0; i < word1.length; i++) { concatenatedWord1 += word1[i]; }<br/>\\n    for (let j = 0; j < word2.length; j++) { concatenatedWord2 += word2[j]; }<br/>\\n    return concatenatedWord1 === concatenatedWord2;<br/><br/>\\n</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>The time complexity is O(n + m), where n is the length of `word1` and m is the length of `word2`.<br/><br/></div>\"}",
