            "question": "{\"content\":\"<style>\\n    /* 2545. */\\n    .text-container {\\n      cursor: pointer;\\n      max-width: 20px;\\n      white-space: normal; /* Allow the text to wrap */\\n      overflow: hidden;\\n      position: relative;\\n      line-height: 1.4; /* Adjust for better readability */\\n      transition: max-width 0.3s ease-in-out, max-height 0.3s ease-in-out; /* Smooth transition for expanding */\\n      max-height: 1.4em; /* Set to line height to show only the first line */\\n    }\\n    .text-container::after {\\n      content: '  ...  ';\\n      position: absolute;\\n      right: 0;\\n      bottom: 0;\\n      color: #d5d5d5;\\n      background:  #f0f0f0;\\n      width: 100%;\\n      text-align: right;\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover {\\n      max-width: 350px; /* Adjust as needed for hover */\\n      max-height: none; /* Allow it to expand to fit content */\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover::after {\\n      display: none; /* Hide the ellipsis on hover */\\n    }\\n    a:link, a:visited {\\n        color: black;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n<span><b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-the-students-by-their-kth-score\\\">2545. Sort the Students by Their Kth Score</a></b></span>\\n<div>Sort students by scores in a specific exam from a matrix of scores.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1. A class contains `m` students and `n` exams.<br/>2. Scores are represented in an `m x n` matrix, `score`, where `score[i][j]` is the score of the `i`th student in the `j`th exam.<br/>3. Scores are unique integers.<br/>4. Given an integer `k`, sort the students based on their scores in the `k`th exam, from highest to lowest.<br/>5. Return the sorted matrix.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>`score = [[10,6,9,1],[7,5,11,2],[4,8,3,15]], k = 2` => `[[7,5,11,2],[10,6,9,1],[4,8,3,15]]`</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>Example 1:<br/><br/>Input: score = [[10,6,9,1],[7,5,11,2],[4,8,3,15]], k = 2<br/>Output: [[7,5,11,2],[10,6,9,1],[4,8,3,15]]<br/>Explanation: In the above diagram, S denotes the student, while E denotes the exam.<br/>- The student with index 1 scored 11 in exam 2, which is the highest score, so they got first place.<br/>- The student with index 0 scored 9 in exam 2, which is the second highest score, so they got second place.<br/>- The student with index 2 scored 3 in exam 2, which is the lowest score, so they got third place.<br/><br/><br/>Example 2:<br/><br/>Input: score = [[3,4],[5,6]], k = 0<br/>Output: [[5,6],[3,4]]<br/>Explanation: In the above diagram, S denotes the student, while E denotes the exam.<br/>- The student with index 1 scored 5 in exam 0, which is the highest score, so they got first place.<br/>- The student with index 0 scored 3 in exam 0, which is the lowest score, so they got second place.<br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>m == score.length<br/>n == score[i].length<br/>1 <= m, n <= 250<br/>1 <= score[i][j] <= 105<br/>score consists of distinct integers.<br/>0 <= k < n<br/></div>\\n<span><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-the-students-by-their-kth-score/solutions/\\\">[1]</a></span>\"}",
            "answer": "{\"content\":\"<b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/sort-the-students-by-their-kth-score/solutions/3086535/one-liner-javascript-solution/\\\">Sorting Algorithm (Array Sorting)</a></b>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>let score = [[...], [...], ...]; // 2D array representing student scores<br/>let k = 0; // Integer representing the exam index to sort by<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>score.sort((a, b) => b[k] - a[k]);</div>\\n<div>1. Use the JavaScript `Array.sort()` method.<br/>2. Sort the rows of the matrix based on the scores in the `k`th exam.<br/>3. Sort in descending order (higher scores first).</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>The solution sorts the matrix rows based on the scores in the `k`th column using a custom comparator in the `sort` method.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>let alternativeScore = [...score]; // Copy of the original score array<br/>alternativeScore.sort((a, b) => a[k] - b[k]); // Alternative sorting in ascending order<br/><br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>O(m log m), where m is the number of students (rows in the matrix), as the sorting algorithm typically has this complexity.<br/><br/></div>\"}",
