            "question": "{\"content\":\"<style>\\n    /* 1431. */\\n    .text-container {\\n      cursor: pointer;\\n      max-width: 20px;\\n      white-space: normal; /* Allow the text to wrap */\\n      overflow: hidden;\\n      position: relative;\\n      line-height: 1.4; /* Adjust for better readability */\\n      transition: max-width 0.3s ease-in-out, max-height 0.3s ease-in-out; /* Smooth transition for expanding */\\n      max-height: 1.4em; /* Set to line height to show only the first line */\\n    }\\n    .text-container::after {\\n      content: '  ...  ';\\n      position: absolute;\\n      right: 0;\\n      bottom: 0;\\n      color: #d5d5d5;\\n      background:  #f0f0f0;\\n      width: 100%;\\n      text-align: right;\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover {\\n      max-width: 350px; /* Adjust as needed for hover */\\n      max-height: none; /* Allow it to expand to fit content */\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover::after {\\n      display: none; /* Hide the ellipsis on hover */\\n    }\\n    a:link, a:visited {\\n        color: black;\\n    }\\n    resize-img. {\\n        max-height:450px;\\n        min-width:350px;\\n        width:auto;\\n        display:block;\\n        margin:auto;\\n        background-color: #131516;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n<span><b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/kids-with-the-greatest-number-of-candies\\\">1431. Kids With the Greatest Number of Candies</a></b></span>\\n<div>The problem involves determining if giving each kid all the extra candies results in them having the greatest number of candies among all kids. We need to return a boolean array indicating this for each kid.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1. We have an array `candies`, where `candies[i]` represents the number of candies the ith kid has.<br/>2. We are also given an integer `extraCandies`, representing the number of additional candies available.<br/>3. The task is to return a boolean array `result` of the same length as `candies`.<br/>4. `result[i]` should be true if giving the ith kid all `extraCandies` makes their total candies the greatest or equal to the greatest among all kids; otherwise, false.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>- [2,3,5,1,3], 3 => [true, true, true, false, true]<br/>- [4,2,1,1,2], 1 => [true, false, false, false, false]</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>Example 1:<br/><br/>Input: candies = [2,3,5,1,3], extraCandies = 3<br/>Output: [true,true,true,false,true] <br/>Explanation: If you give all extraCandies to:<br/>- Kid 1, they will have 2 + 3 = 5 candies, which is the greatest among the kids.<br/>- Kid 2, they will have 3 + 3 = 6 candies, which is the greatest among the kids.<br/>- Kid 3, they will have 5 + 3 = 8 candies, which is the greatest among the kids.<br/>- Kid 4, they will have 1 + 3 = 4 candies, which is not the greatest among the kids.<br/>- Kid 5, they will have 3 + 3 = 6 candies, which is the greatest among the kids.<br/><br/><br/>Example 2:<br/><br/>Input: candies = [4,2,1,1,2], extraCandies = 1<br/>Output: [true,false,false,false,false] <br/>Explanation: There is only 1 extra candy.<br/>Kid 1 will always have the greatest number of candies, even if a different kid is given the extra candy.<br/><br/><br/>Example 3:<br/><br/>Input: candies = [12,1,12], extraCandies = 10<br/>Output: [true,false,true]<br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>n == candies.length<br/>2 <= n <= 100<br/>1 <= candies[i] <= 100<br/>1 <= extraCandies <= 50<br/></div>\\n<span>\\n<a href=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=pXAvwRyJ0dw&pp=ygUuMTQzMS4gS2lkcyBXaXRoIHRoZSBHcmVhdGVzdCBOdW1iZXIgb2YgQ2FuZGllcw%3D%3D\\\"><svg xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" x=\\\"0px\\\" y=\\\"0px\\\" width=\\\"30\\\" height=\\\"25\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 48 48\\\">\\n<path fill=\\\"#FF3D00\\\" d=\\\"M43.2,33.9c-0.4,2.1-2.1,3.7-4.2,4c-3.3,0.5-8.8,1.1-15,1.1c-6.1,0-11.6-0.6-15-1.1c-2.1-0.3-3.8-1.9-4.2-4C4.4,31.6,4,28.2,4,24c0-4.2,0.4-7.6,0.8-9.9c0.4-2.1,2.1-3.7,4.2-4C12.3,9.6,17.8,9,24,9c6.2,0,11.6,0.6,15,1.1c2.1,0.3,3.8,1.9,4.2,4c0.4,2.3,0.9,5.7,0.9,9.9C44,28.2,43.6,31.6,43.2,33.9z\\\"></path><path fill=\\\"#FFF\\\" d=\\\"M20 31L20 17 32 24z\\\"></path>\\n</svg></a>\\nJS: <a href=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=zx0b1tiVvgA&pp=ygU4MTQzMS4gS2lkcyBXaXRoIHRoZSBHcmVhdGVzdCBOdW1iZXIgb2YgQ2FuZGllc2phdmFzY3JpcHQ%3D\\\"><svg xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" x=\\\"0px\\\" y=\\\"0px\\\" width=\\\"30\\\" height=\\\"25\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 48 48\\\">\\n<path fill=\\\"#FF3D00\\\" d=\\\"M43.2,33.9c-0.4,2.1-2.1,3.7-4.2,4c-3.3,0.5-8.8,1.1-15,1.1c-6.1,0-11.6-0.6-15-1.1c-2.1-0.3-3.8-1.9-4.2-4C4.4,31.6,4,28.2,4,24c0-4.2,0.4-7.6,0.8-9.9c0.4-2.1,2.1-3.7,4.2-4C12.3,9.6,17.8,9,24,9c6.2,0,11.6,0.6,15,1.1c2.1,0.3,3.8,1.9,4.2,4c0.4,2.3,0.9,5.7,0.9,9.9C44,28.2,43.6,31.6,43.2,33.9z\\\"></path><path fill=\\\"#FFF\\\" d=\\\"M20 31L20 17 32 24z\\\"></path>\\n</svg></a>\\nSolutions:<a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/kids-with-the-greatest-number-of-candies/solutions/\\\">[1]</a></span>\"}",
            "answer": "{\"content\":\"<b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/kids-with-the-greatest-number-of-candies/solutions/631287/javascript-easy-to-understand-from-common-to-1-line/\\\">The algorithm uses array manipulation and comparison.</a></b>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>const kidsWithCandies = (candies, extraCandies, max = Math.max(...candies)) => {<br/>    // ... rest of the code ...<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>const kidsWithCandies = (candies, extraCandies, max = Math.max(...candies)) => <br/>    candies.map(candy => candy + extraCandies >= max);</div>\\n<div>1. Calculate the maximum number of candies any kid has initially using `Math.max(...candies)`.<br/>2. Map over the `candies` array.<br/>3. For each element in the array (each kid's candy count), add `extraCandies` to their current count.<br/>4. Compare this sum to the maximum count calculated. If it's greater than or equal to the max, return `true`; otherwise, `false`.<br/>5. This results in a new array where each element corresponds to whether each kid would have the greatest number of candies after receiving the extra candies.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>The provided solution efficiently computes the result in a single pass using the `map` function. It first finds the maximum candy count among all kids and then checks for each kid if adding `extraCandies` to their count reaches or exceeds this maximum.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>const kidsWithCandies = (candies, extraCandies) => {<br/>    const maxCandy = Math.max(...candies);<br/>    return candies.map(candy => candy + extraCandies >= maxCandy);<br/>};<br/><br/>This alternative solution separates the computation of `maxCandy` from the function parameters for clarity. It retains the same functionality and time complexity as the provided solution.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>The time complexity is O(n), where n is the number of kids. This is because we iterate over the `candies` array once to compute the maximum and once more to generate the result array.</div>\"}",
