            "question": "{\"content\":\"<style>\\n    /* 1561. */\\n    .text-container {\\n      cursor: pointer;\\n      max-width: 20px;\\n      white-space: normal; /* Allow the text to wrap */\\n      overflow: hidden;\\n      position: relative;\\n      line-height: 1.4; /* Adjust for better readability */\\n      transition: max-width 0.3s ease-in-out, max-height 0.3s ease-in-out; /* Smooth transition for expanding */\\n      max-height: 1.4em; /* Set to line height to show only the first line */\\n    }\\n    .text-container::after {\\n      content: '  ...  ';\\n      position: absolute;\\n      right: 0;\\n      bottom: 0;\\n      color: #d5d5d5;\\n      background:  #f0f0f0;\\n      width: 100%;\\n      text-align: right;\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover {\\n      max-width: 350px; /* Adjust as needed for hover */\\n      max-height: none; /* Allow it to expand to fit content */\\n    }\\n    .text-container:hover::after {\\n      display: none; /* Hide the ellipsis on hover */\\n    }\\n    a:link, a:visited {\\n        color: black;\\n    }\\n    resize-img. {\\n        max-height:450px;\\n        min-width:350px;\\n        width:auto;\\n        display:block;\\n        margin:auto;\\n        background-color: #131516;\\n    }\\n  </style>\\n<span><b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-number-of-coins-you-can-get\\\">1561. Maximum Number of Coins You Can Get</a></b></span>\\n<div>The problem involves maximizing the number of coins you can have by selecting from 3n piles of coins. In each step, three piles are chosen, with the maximum pile going to Alice, the next maximum to you, and the smallest to Bob. This process is repeated until all piles are chosen.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>1. You are given an array `piles` representing piles of coins.<br/>2. The array length is 3n, and you need to select coins in a way to maximize your share.<br/>3. In each selection, the largest pile goes to Alice, the second largest to you, and the smallest to Bob.<br/>4. You need to find the maximum number of coins you can have after all selections.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>- `piles = [2,4,1,2,7,8]` => `9`<br/>- `piles = [2,4,5]` => `4`<br/>- `piles = [9,8,7,6,5,1,2,3,4]` => `18`</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>Example 1:<br/><br/>Input: piles = [2,4,1,2,7,8]<br/>Output: 9<br/>Explanation: Choose the triplet (2, 7, 8), Alice Pick the pile with 8 coins, you the pile with 7 coins and Bob the last one.<br/>Choose the triplet (1, 2, 4), Alice Pick the pile with 4 coins, you the pile with 2 coins and Bob the last one.<br/>The maximum number of coins which you can have are: 7 + 2 = 9.<br/>On the other hand if we choose this arrangement (1, 2, 8), (2, 4, 7) you only get 2 + 4 = 6 coins which is not optimal.<br/><br/><br/>Example 2:<br/><br/>Input: piles = [2,4,5]<br/>Output: 4<br/><br/><br/>Example 3:<br/><br/>Input: piles = [9,8,7,6,5,1,2,3,4]<br/>Output: 18<br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>3 <= piles.length <= 105<br/>piles.length % 3 == 0<br/>1 <= piles[i] <= 104<br/></div>\\n<span>\\n<a href=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hPe9Z3TiUrA&pp=ygUpMTU2MS4gTWF4aW11bSBOdW1iZXIgb2YgQ29pbnMgWW91IENhbiBHZXQ%3D\\\"><svg xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" x=\\\"0px\\\" y=\\\"0px\\\" width=\\\"30\\\" height=\\\"25\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 48 48\\\">\\n<path fill=\\\"#FF3D00\\\" d=\\\"M43.2,33.9c-0.4,2.1-2.1,3.7-4.2,4c-3.3,0.5-8.8,1.1-15,1.1c-6.1,0-11.6-0.6-15-1.1c-2.1-0.3-3.8-1.9-4.2-4C4.4,31.6,4,28.2,4,24c0-4.2,0.4-7.6,0.8-9.9c0.4-2.1,2.1-3.7,4.2-4C12.3,9.6,17.8,9,24,9c6.2,0,11.6,0.6,15,1.1c2.1,0.3,3.8,1.9,4.2,4c0.4,2.3,0.9,5.7,0.9,9.9C44,28.2,43.6,31.6,43.2,33.9z\\\"></path><path fill=\\\"#FFF\\\" d=\\\"M20 31L20 17 32 24z\\\"></path>\\n</svg></a>\\nJS: <a href=\\\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hPe9Z3TiUrA&pp=ygUzMTU2MS4gTWF4aW11bSBOdW1iZXIgb2YgQ29pbnMgWW91IENhbiBHZXRqYXZhc2NyaXB0\\\"><svg xmlns=\\\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\\\" x=\\\"0px\\\" y=\\\"0px\\\" width=\\\"30\\\" height=\\\"25\\\" viewBox=\\\"0 0 48 48\\\">\\n<path fill=\\\"#FF3D00\\\" d=\\\"M43.2,33.9c-0.4,2.1-2.1,3.7-4.2,4c-3.3,0.5-8.8,1.1-15,1.1c-6.1,0-11.6-0.6-15-1.1c-2.1-0.3-3.8-1.9-4.2-4C4.4,31.6,4,28.2,4,24c0-4.2,0.4-7.6,0.8-9.9c0.4-2.1,2.1-3.7,4.2-4C12.3,9.6,17.8,9,24,9c6.2,0,11.6,0.6,15,1.1c2.1,0.3,3.8,1.9,4.2,4c0.4,2.3,0.9,5.7,0.9,9.9C44,28.2,43.6,31.6,43.2,33.9z\\\"></path><path fill=\\\"#FFF\\\" d=\\\"M20 31L20 17 32 24z\\\"></path>\\n</svg></a>\\nSolutions:<a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-number-of-coins-you-can-get/solutions/\\\">[1]</a></span>\"}",
            "answer": "{\"content\":\"<b><a href=\\\"https://leetcode.com/problems/maximum-number-of-coins-you-can-get/solutions/1518949/javascript-solutions-2-18-38-371ms-also-a-1-liner/\\\">The solution involves <b>sorting</b> and <b>selective summing</b>.</a></b>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>var maxCoins = function(piles) {<br/>    let result = 0;<br/>    let len = (piles.length * (2/3)); // Calculate 2/3 of the array length<br/>    piles.sort((a, b) => b - a); // Sort the array in descending order<br/>    // ...<br/><br/></div>\\n<div>var maxCoins = function(piles) {<br/>    piles.sort((a, b) => b - a);<br/>    let result = 0;<br/>    for (let i = 0; i ＜ piles.length / 3; i++) {<br/>        result += piles[i * 2 + 1];<br/>    }<br/>    return result;<br/>};</div>\\n<div>1. Initialize a variable `result` to 0 to store the total number of coins.<br/>2. Calculate `len` as 2/3 of the length of `piles`, because Bob will always get the smallest third.<br/>3. Sort `piles` in descending order.<br/>4. Use a for loop to iterate through `piles`, starting at index 1 and incrementing by 2 (to skip Alice's share and take every second pile starting from the second-largest).<br/>5. In each iteration, add the value of `piles[i]` to `result`.<br/>6. Return `result` as the total number of coins you can have.</div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/><br/><br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>var maxCoins = function(piles) {<br/>    let result = 0, len = (piles.length * (2/3));<br/>    piles.sort((a, b) => b - a);<br/>    for(let i = 1; i ＜ len; i += 2){ result += piles[i]; }<br/>    return result;<br/>};</br></br>The solution smartly sorts the piles in descending order and then iteratively adds every second pile starting from the second pile in the sorted array. This approach ensures that you always get the second-largest pile in each selection, maximizing the total coins.<br/><br/></div>\\n<div class=\\\"text-container\\\"><br/>The time complexity of the solution is <b>O(n log n)</b>, where `n` is the number of piles. The sorting step dominates the time complexity.<br/><br/>---</div>\"}",
